subtask_prior_summary_user:
Task goal:
{task_name}

Skill context:
- stage_idx: {stage_idx}
- skill_idx: {skill_idx}
- skill_description: {skill_description}
- object_id: {object_id}
- manuipation_object_id: {manuipation_object_id}
- frame_duration: {frame_duration}

Minimum final item count per list field: {prior_min_items}

Preliminary prior JSON from sampled frames:
{preliminary_prior_json}

Consolidate the preliminary prior into a precise final skill prior. Requirements:
- Remove duplicate or near-duplicate items; do not pad by rephrasing the same idea.
- Use frame_state_memory from sampled frames to preserve temporal state, robot-object relation, and uncertainty when forming final criteria.
- Replace vague phrases with concrete visible attributes. Do not use standalone generic references such as "indicator", "central button", "button", "it", or "this target". Every list item must fully name the target object and target part, such as "the radio small circular power button on the top control area".
- Each final list field must contain at least {prior_min_items} non-duplicate high-value items: completion_conditions, required_visual_evidence, state_transition_evidence, negative_conditions, common_false_positives, and ambiguous_cases.
- Items in the same list must be diverse and cover different evidence angles when possible: target appearance, target part location, relative size/count, robot gripper contact or release, before/after visual state, object support or spatial relation, occlusion, glare/reflection, motion blur, color ambiguity, and viewpoint ambiguity.
- Include color, shape, location, relative size, and count when visible.
- Completion conditions must describe the completed result state, not just the robot action. At least one completion condition must be a decisive visual gate that combines the fully named target part with the final visible state.
- Required visual evidence must be directly observable.
- State transition evidence must name the before and after states when applicable, such as "the same small circular power button changes from red to green".
- Negative conditions and false positives must be specific enough to block wrong completed labels.

Return JSON with these keys:
{
  "subtask_name": "short executable subtask name",
  "target_visual_description": {
    "target_object": "specific object name",
    "target_part": "specific manipulated part or whole object",
    "color": "specific visible color(s)",
    "shape": "specific visible shape",
    "position": "specific location on object or in workspace",
    "size": "relative visible size",
    "count": "number of relevant targets"
  },
  "completion_conditions": ["specific semantic postcondition with concrete target state"],
  "required_visual_evidence": ["specific visual evidence with color/shape/location/count when visible"],
  "state_transition_evidence": ["specific before-to-after visual change, if applicable"],
  "negative_conditions": ["specific visible condition that means the skill is not completed"],
  "common_false_positives": ["specific visual pattern that can look completed but is not sufficient"],
  "ambiguous_cases": ["specific case that should be labeled no_for_sure or handled conservatively"]
}

